Non-Celiac Gluten Sensitivity
Integrative & Functional medicine
GI distress following gluten consumption, Chronic abdominal bloating/altered bowel habits, Brain fog/migrating joint aching, Negative coeliac serology/normal duodenal biopsy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-028
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, painful abdominal distension, intractable flatulence, and debilitating brain fog requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute bowel obstruction or acute central neurological event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing profound, painful abdominal swelling, highly irregular bowel habits, and severe cognitive sluggishness (unable to focus or complete tasks). Urgent on-site physical and neurological assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute bowel obstruction, acute ischemic colitis, or an acute cerebrovascular event, preventing irreversible bowel necrosis or neurological deficit prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is swollen so tight it physically hurts to breathe, and my brain is so foggy I can't even remember what I was doing an hour ago."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a highly delayed, insidious onset of distinct severe gastrointestinal symptoms that began roughly 48 hours after consuming a large pasta meal containing gluten. The patient describes chronic, recurrent severe generalized abdominal bloating and profound severe flatulence, which has acutely worsened today into painful distension. Bowel habits are currently highly irregular, distinctly alternating between constipation and loose stools today. The patient also complains of severe brain fog, prominent distinct cognitive sluggishness, and significantly poor concentration; the patient struggles to articulate timelines during the interview. Furthermore, the patient reports widespread vague migrating joint aching, distinct profound severe muscle stiffness, and profound generalized fatigue. Historically, the patient has had completely negative coeliac serology (negative tTG-IgA) and a distinctly normal targeted duodenal biopsy.
Functional Status: Patient is seated, appearing lethargic, frequently rubbing the abdomen, unable to perform basic tasks due to cognitive fog.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high distress regarding the sudden cognitive decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears physically uncomfortable, lethargic, with noticeably delayed verbal responses and blunted affect.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves, normal symmetric motor strength, and normal reflexes. While processing speed is remarkably slow (brain fog), orientation is intact and there is no expressive or receptive aphasia, focal weakness, or facial droop, ruling out an acute cerebrovascular event. Abdominal exam shows prominent, tense generalized distension. The abdomen is highly tympanitic to percussion diffusely. Palpation reveals widespread, mild discomfort secondary to stretching, but the abdomen is entirely soft without localized tenderness, true rigidity, or rebound peritonism. Bowel sounds are normal to hyperactive. Musculoskeletal exam shows diffuse, vague tenderness without localized joint effusion. The presentation fits the diagnostic tetrad of GI distress following gluten consumption, chronic abdominal bloating, brain fog/migrating joint aching, and a history of negative coeliac tests.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting in a quiet environment. Empty pasta packaging visible in the kitchen recycling.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter anti-gas medications (Simethicone) with no relief; no active prescription medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Non-Celiac Gluten Sensitivity - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, non-tinkling bowel sounds, absence of severe colicky pain, continued passage of flatus, and no history of intractable bilious vomiting.

Acute Cerebrovascular Accident (Stroke/TIA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of focal, lateralizing neurological signs and intact (though slow) speech and comprehension.

Undiagnosed Celiac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Documented history of negative coeliac serology and a normal duodenal biopsy, strictly pointing toward non-celiac sensitivity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe cognitive slowing (brain fog), massive abdominal distension, and diffuse systemic pain. Telephone triage correctly identified the emergent need to evaluate for an acute stroke (given the profound cognitive changes) or an acute surgical abdomen/obstruction. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to perform a formal stroke screen (FAST assessment), auscultate the abdomen, and evaluate for peritonism. The physical exclusion of focal neurological deficits and a rigid, silent abdomen confirmed immediate medical stability. However, the severity of the cognitive impairment, the debilitating abdominal pain from distension, and the immense distress of the patient dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of profound psychological distress, potential diagnostic delay of a subtle ischemic event, and severe unmanaged pain. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the absence of an acute stroke or bowel obstruction. Advised a strict, simple whole-foods diet (e.g., plain rice, steamed vegetables) for 48 hours to reduce the digestive and immune burden. Prescribed a short course of Paracetamol 1g QID PRN for joint analgesia. Recommend routine GP follow-up for ongoing dietary management and potential dietitian referral.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage chronic immune reactivity and extreme bloating, initiate a strict elimination and gut-rest framework. Action: Implement a strict 100% elimination of all gluten-containing grains (wheat, barley, rye) indefinitely. Consequence: Removing the highly reactive dietary antigen physically halts the systemic inflammatory cascade, directly rewarding the patient with a clearance of the severe brain fog, migrating joint pain, and profound bloating. Action: Drink a warm cup of peppermint or ginger tea immediately after meals. Consequence: The essential oils in these herbs act as direct carminatives, physically relaxing the smooth muscle of the gut and explicitly rewarding the patient by facilitating the safe passage of trapped gas.
